From 379d39c94ff620b2b2f174726d96a9b8c917384e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 21:02:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix service-health correctness, configurability and query
 safety

Follow-up to #3. The feature was sound in shape but could not be used
outside one metric profile, and several signals could report a confident
wrong severity.

Configurability
- Wire servicehealth.Config through env vars, Terraform and the raw
  manifest. It was previously constructed as a zero value, so the
  metric names, label names and all ten thresholds were unreachable and
  every deployment was pinned to the ASP.NET Core defaults.
- Reject configuration that is set but unusable (malformed identifier,
  critical <= warning, unparseable number) at startup instead of
  silently substituting a default. The old thresholdsOr could invert a
  pair: warning 10 / critical 8 became warning 10 / critical 5.

Query safety
- Give range and instant queries their own client and timeout
  (BEHOLDR_PROMETHEUS_QUERY_TIMEOUT, default 30s). They previously
  inherited the 5s connectivity-check client, which a multi-week range
  query will routinely exceed.
- Select the Prometheus provider by name rather than providers[0].
  Reordering that slice would have sent the Elasticsearch API key to
  Prometheus's query API.
- Cache reports per (workload, window), collapse concurrent identical
  requests into one evaluation, and bound in-flight queries. The UI
  polls per open tab and Beholdr has no auth of its own.
- Drop the response cap from 16 MiB to 2 MiB; several queries buffer
  concurrently against a 512Mi limit.
- Classify Prometheus's documented errorType enum into an actionable
  closed vocabulary instead of one generic "query failed".

Correctness
- Score severity from an instant query, not the last range point. The
  old behaviour made severity depend on the selected chart window: at a
  1h step a spike from the last ten minutes was invisible on the 21d
  view.
- Pin scoring to the workload's live pod names. "^name(-.+)?$", used for
  every non-Deployment workload, matched sibling services' pods.
  Charts keep a tightened name-shape selector for rollout history.
- Score CPU against limits by default, configurable to requests.
  Requests are a scheduling floor; 80/95 against them marks healthy
  bursty services critical forever.
- Distinguish "metric absent for this workload" from "query failed".
  A service with no memory limit no longer reads as unknown forever,
  while a failed query still cannot make a service look green.
- Suppress the week-before overlay past 7d, where offset 1w overlaps
  the current series instead of comparing against it.
- Exclude Job pods from the failing-pod count; a Pod that failed weeks
  ago reports phase="Failed" for as long as the object exists.
- Error on unexpected extra series instead of scoring series[0].
- Omit the warning threshold for single-replica workloads, where it was
  equal to critical.

UI and docs
- Drive formatting from the API's unit and state fields rather than a
  second switch on the signal key.
- Document the metric profile, thresholds, kube-state-metrics
  dependency and the pod-selection limitation.
- Add .gitattributes; the repository currently rewrites every line on a
  Windows checkout. Run `git add --renormalize .` once after applying.

